    <string name="guide_title">Measure guide</string>
    <string name="guide_subtitle">FOUR HABITS OF A GOOD WORKROOM</string>
    <string name="guide_track_title">MEASURING A TRACK</string>
    <string name="guide_track_body">Measure the track itself, from end stop to end stop, and ignore the window behind it. Add the two returns where the track bends back to the wall, and add the overlap where the two curtains cross in the middle. Hold the tape level and read it twice.</string>
    <string name="guide_pole_title">MEASURING A POLE</string>
    <string name="guide_pole_body">Measure between the finials, not over them, and not to the brackets. Eyelet and wave headings travel along the whole pole, so the finials are the real end stops. Note the pole diameter as well, because large eyelets need a matching ring size.</string>
    <string name="guide_drop_title">CHOOSING YOUR DROP</string>
    <string name="guide_drop_body">Measure from the top of the track or from the eye of the ring down to where the curtain should finish. Sill length stops about one centimetre above the sill, floor length clears the floor by one or two centimetres, and a puddle adds five to fifteen centimetres instead.</string>
    <string name="guide_fullness_title">FULLNESS AND PATTERN</string>
    <string name="guide_fullness_body">Fullness is how much flat fabric you gather into the finished width. Two times is quiet and tailored, two and a half times reads generous. A patterned fabric must start every width at the same point in the repeat, so each cut length is rounded up to a whole number of repeats.</string>

